1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,590
Another battery caught fire this week.

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:04,870
A power bank on a plane.

3
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:07,850
A scooter left charging in a stairwell.

4
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:10,150
Sometimes an entire building.

5
00:00:10,934 --> 00:00:12,634
And the headline is always the same.

6
00:00:12,714 --> 00:00:15,884
Lithium-ion batteries are dangerous.

7
00:00:16,835 --> 00:00:18,655
There is something odd about that.

8
00:00:18,735 --> 00:00:20,845
Because there is one in your pocket.

9
00:00:21,075 --> 00:00:22,485
One in your laptop.

10
00:00:23,155 --> 00:00:24,765
Probably one parked outside.

11
00:00:25,790 --> 00:00:28,250
Billions of them, sitting
in drawers and bags

12
00:00:28,330 --> 00:00:31,220
and walls, doing nothing
dramatic whatsoever.

13
00:00:32,073 --> 00:00:33,303
So which is it?

14
00:00:33,873 --> 00:00:37,963
Did we quietly hand a dangerous
technology to almost everybody on Earth?

15
00:00:40,263 --> 00:00:42,073
Here is the short answer.

16
00:00:42,743 --> 00:00:44,893
A lithium cell carries its own fuel.

17
00:00:45,663 --> 00:00:47,733
The flammable part isn't in the room.

18
00:00:48,223 --> 00:00:50,193
It is sealed inside the battery.

19
00:00:50,946 --> 00:00:54,346
That is why a battery fire
is so hard to smother, and

20
00:00:54,426 --> 00:00:57,436
why it can start again an hour
after you think it is out.

21
00:00:58,231 --> 00:01:00,641
But the short answer is the boring one.

22
00:01:01,271 --> 00:01:04,761
Almost every one of those billions
never does anything of the sort.

23
00:01:05,561 --> 00:01:07,851
So the question is
narrower than the headline.

24
00:01:08,761 --> 00:01:10,311
What has to go wrong first?

25
00:01:12,785 --> 00:01:14,995
It helps to remember how new this is.

26
00:01:15,925 --> 00:01:19,635
Sony sold the first commercial
lithium-ion cell in 1991.

27
00:01:20,605 --> 00:01:23,805
Lithium is the lightest metal
there is, so the chemistry

28
00:01:23,885 --> 00:01:26,955
stores a great deal of energy
for very little weight.

29
00:01:27,699 --> 00:01:32,959
And then it got cheap. Between
2010 and 2023, the average price of

30
00:01:33,039 --> 00:01:35,629
a battery pack fell by
almost ninety percent.

31
00:01:36,607 --> 00:01:39,097
Light and cheap is a powerful combination.

32
00:01:39,887 --> 00:01:43,857
It is why one technology can sit
inside a phone and underneath a car.

33
00:01:46,307 --> 00:01:49,177
From the outside, a phone
battery is deeply boring.

34
00:01:49,967 --> 00:01:51,537
A flat silver rectangle.

35
00:01:52,443 --> 00:01:54,713
Inside, it is not boring at all.

36
00:01:55,504 --> 00:01:57,314
There is a positive electrode.

37
00:01:57,404 --> 00:01:58,964
A negative electrode.

38
00:01:59,044 --> 00:02:02,194
And a liquid electrolyte between them.

39
00:02:02,857 --> 00:02:07,667
Charging pushes lithium ions through that
liquid, from one electrode to the other.

40
00:02:08,457 --> 00:02:10,747
Using the phone lets them drift back.

41
00:02:11,489 --> 00:02:13,589
The electrons cannot take that route.

42
00:02:13,669 --> 00:02:15,729
So they go the long way round...

43
00:02:15,809 --> 00:02:17,359
through your phone.

44
00:02:18,029 --> 00:02:20,059
That is the part we actually wanted.

45
00:02:21,005 --> 00:02:24,475
And in the middle sits the
component nobody ever thinks about.

46
00:02:25,304 --> 00:02:29,734
The separator. A plastic film that
holds the two electrodes apart.

47
00:02:30,650 --> 00:02:34,260
In a modern cell it is typically
under twenty micrometres thick.

48
00:02:35,170 --> 00:02:37,100
A human hair is around seventy.

49
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:39,430
So that is the design.

50
00:02:39,780 --> 00:02:42,900
A great deal of energy.
A flammable liquid.

51
00:02:42,980 --> 00:02:45,670
And a film keeping the halves apart.

52
00:02:46,530 --> 00:02:48,920
Enormously useful. Not very forgiving.

53
00:02:51,390 --> 00:02:54,180
Batteries do not usually
burn of their own accord.

54
00:02:54,910 --> 00:02:56,840
Something has to go wrong first.

55
00:02:57,696 --> 00:03:00,386
Crushed. Overheated. Overcharged.

56
00:03:00,716 --> 00:03:03,666
Or simply built badly
on a Tuesday afternoon.

57
00:03:04,495 --> 00:03:06,745
The worst case is an internal short.

58
00:03:07,515 --> 00:03:10,345
The two sides meet somewhere
they were never meant to meet.

59
00:03:11,142 --> 00:03:14,652
And now the energy of the whole
cell pours through one tiny spot.

60
00:03:15,102 --> 00:03:16,892
That spot gets hot.

61
00:03:17,683 --> 00:03:19,353
And here is the awkward part.

62
00:03:19,883 --> 00:03:21,373
The separator is plastic.

63
00:03:22,063 --> 00:03:24,073
Plastic does not do well when heated.

64
00:03:24,929 --> 00:03:29,019
Heat it enough and it softens, shrinks
away from the hot spot, and gives way.

65
00:03:29,529 --> 00:03:31,239
So the hole gets bigger.

66
00:03:32,089 --> 00:03:33,999
A bigger hole carries more current.

67
00:03:34,229 --> 00:03:36,219
More current makes more heat.

68
00:03:36,889 --> 00:03:38,599
More heat opens a bigger hole.

69
00:03:39,399 --> 00:03:42,219
Meanwhile the heat is starting
chemical reactions in the

70
00:03:42,299 --> 00:03:45,609
cell, and those reactions
give off heat of their own.

71
00:03:46,301 --> 00:03:47,681
Heat makes reactions.

72
00:03:47,761 --> 00:03:49,711
Reactions make heat.

73
00:03:49,861 --> 00:03:52,131
Nothing in that loop slows it down.

74
00:03:52,886 --> 00:03:54,836
It has a name. Thermal runaway.

75
00:03:57,396 --> 00:04:01,986
The battery stops being a power supply and
becomes a heater that is feeding itself.

76
00:04:02,911 --> 00:04:05,881
The electrolyte boils. Pressure builds.

77
00:04:06,751 --> 00:04:09,981
The cell splits open, and what
comes out is hot and flammable.

78
00:04:10,648 --> 00:04:15,158
And in some chemistries, the breaking
electrode gives off oxygen as it goes.

79
00:04:16,013 --> 00:04:19,083
Which is the part people
find genuinely unnerving.

80
00:04:19,973 --> 00:04:23,083
In those cells, the fire has
brought some of its own air.

81
00:04:23,770 --> 00:04:25,580
Smothering it does very little.

82
00:04:26,470 --> 00:04:31,040
Firefighters mostly use water to cool what
is around it, and wait for it to finish.

83
00:04:31,868 --> 00:04:33,798
Because cells are rarely alone.

84
00:04:34,508 --> 00:04:37,618
In a bike or a car they sit
shoulder to shoulder, in packs.

85
00:04:38,537 --> 00:04:41,437
And a cell in thermal
runaway is a very hot lump

86
00:04:41,517 --> 00:04:43,987
of metal pressed against
the next one along.

87
00:04:44,777 --> 00:04:46,997
That is the difference
between a phone that has a bad

88
00:04:47,077 --> 00:04:50,587
afternoon and a fire that
takes the stairwell with it.

89
00:04:51,651 --> 00:04:53,251
By the way, if you'd like to see where the

90
00:04:53,331 --> 00:04:56,061
next question takes us,
consider subscribing.

91
00:04:56,911 --> 00:04:58,401
It really helps the channel.

92
00:05:01,171 --> 00:05:03,301
So. Are they dangerous? Yes.

93
00:05:04,190 --> 00:05:06,240
And that answer is almost useless.

94
00:05:07,187 --> 00:05:08,797
A chainsaw is dangerous.

95
00:05:09,487 --> 00:05:11,077
A gas hob is dangerous.

96
00:05:11,887 --> 00:05:14,437
The question was never
whether a thing can hurt you.

97
00:05:15,117 --> 00:05:16,807
It is how often it does.

98
00:05:18,309 --> 00:05:21,299
And that is where headlines
quietly mislead us.

99
00:05:21,925 --> 00:05:24,315
Here is what rare actually looks like.

100
00:05:24,965 --> 00:05:27,885
In 2016, Samsung recalled two and a half

101
00:05:27,965 --> 00:05:30,515
million phones because
they were catching fire.

102
00:05:31,281 --> 00:05:34,151
The recall cost Samsung more
than five billion dollars.

103
00:05:35,303 --> 00:05:37,643
The number of incidents
reported in America was

104
00:05:37,723 --> 00:05:41,633
ninety-two, out of roughly
a million phones sold there.

105
00:05:42,289 --> 00:05:44,139
That is about one in ten thousand.

106
00:05:45,479 --> 00:05:47,699
One in ten thousand was
enough to end a flagship

107
00:05:47,779 --> 00:05:50,409
phone and get it banned from aeroplanes.

108
00:05:52,869 --> 00:05:56,899
Now hold that number in your head
and count the batteries around you.

109
00:05:57,530 --> 00:06:02,340
When one fails on an aeroplane, or in a
block of flats, everybody hears about it.

110
00:06:03,023 --> 00:06:07,073
The ones that charged quietly on a
bedside table last night are not news.

111
00:06:07,941 --> 00:06:10,831
We see the failures. We
never see the denominator.

112
00:06:13,851 --> 00:06:16,221
Which turns the question
into a better one.

113
00:06:16,751 --> 00:06:20,401
Not are lithium batteries
safe. Safe enough for what?

114
00:06:21,204 --> 00:06:24,374
A phone battery holds
about a dozen watt-hours.

115
00:06:24,984 --> 00:06:27,572
An electric bike, around five hundred.

116
00:06:28,022 --> 00:06:30,492
An electric car carries tens of thousands.

117
00:06:31,502 --> 00:06:34,812
The same family of chemistry,
entirely different consequences.

118
00:06:36,571 --> 00:06:38,561
And the cell is not the whole battery.

119
00:06:39,411 --> 00:06:42,761
A proper pack has electronics
watching it the entire time.

120
00:06:43,495 --> 00:06:45,485
Voltage, current, temperature.

121
00:06:45,675 --> 00:06:50,145
And the authority to cut the battery
off when the numbers stop making sense.

122
00:06:51,017 --> 00:06:52,947
So a battery is really a system.

123
00:06:53,857 --> 00:06:57,907
Cells, sensors, software, a case,
and the charger you plug it into.

124
00:06:58,603 --> 00:07:00,933
Which means any one of them can ruin it.

125
00:07:01,686 --> 00:07:03,376
A flaw in the separator.

126
00:07:03,846 --> 00:07:07,336
A speck of metal in an
electrode. A bad weld.

127
00:07:07,886 --> 00:07:10,936
A protection circuit left
out to save a few pence.

128
00:07:11,751 --> 00:07:13,321
And none of that is visible.

129
00:07:14,171 --> 00:07:15,771
The cheap pack and the careful pack look

130
00:07:15,851 --> 00:07:18,781
identical, right up until
one of them doesn't.

131
00:07:20,931 --> 00:07:24,081
Which is where New York becomes
the interesting example.

132
00:07:24,888 --> 00:07:27,228
In 2023, the city recorded two hundred and

133
00:07:27,308 --> 00:07:30,578
sixty-eight fires started
by these batteries.

134
00:07:31,548 --> 00:07:33,158
Eighteen people died.

135
00:07:33,967 --> 00:07:36,197
Most of it traced back to one place.

136
00:07:36,567 --> 00:07:39,247
Cheap replacement packs
for electric bikes and

137
00:07:39,327 --> 00:07:42,237
scooters, often rebuilt
from salvaged cells.

138
00:07:43,153 --> 00:07:46,003
So the city did something
distinctly unglamorous.

139
00:07:46,853 --> 00:07:51,123
From that September, selling a pack that
had not been certified became illegal.

140
00:07:51,998 --> 00:07:53,408
The timing is striking.

141
00:07:54,138 --> 00:07:58,428
By 2025 the annual death toll
had fallen from eighteen to one.

142
00:07:59,157 --> 00:08:01,087
The chemistry had not changed.

143
00:08:01,717 --> 00:08:04,827
What had changed was which
batteries were reaching the market.

144
00:08:07,497 --> 00:08:09,567
So why not simply make them safe?

145
00:08:10,473 --> 00:08:12,883
Because we are asking
for everything at once.

146
00:08:13,713 --> 00:08:16,743
Small, light, cheap, powerful,
quick to charge and long-lived.

147
00:08:17,974 --> 00:08:21,134
And the more energy we pack
into a small space, the more

148
00:08:21,214 --> 00:08:24,184
there is to release when
something finally goes wrong.

149
00:08:24,875 --> 00:08:27,405
That trade-off is not
an engineering failure.

150
00:08:27,975 --> 00:08:30,325
It is the entire point of the technology.

151
00:08:33,385 --> 00:08:36,595
So what comes next is mostly
more of the same, done better.

152
00:08:37,705 --> 00:08:40,365
Better separators, better
cooling, better ways

153
00:08:40,445 --> 00:08:42,775
to catch a flaw before
it leaves the factory.

154
00:08:43,617 --> 00:08:46,427
Some chemistries already
trade energy for calm.

155
00:08:47,437 --> 00:08:49,977
Lithium iron phosphate
stores less per kilogram

156
00:08:50,057 --> 00:08:52,487
and is much harder to push into runaway.

157
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:56,430
Which is why it keeps turning
up where weight matters least.

158
00:08:57,420 --> 00:09:00,390
A battery bolted to the ground
does not care what it weighs.

159
00:09:01,845 --> 00:09:03,555
Solid-state batteries get the headlines.

160
00:09:04,145 --> 00:09:09,095
Swap the flammable liquid for a solid, and
the part that burns is simply not there.

161
00:09:09,767 --> 00:09:13,977
In principle. Those two words are
doing an enormous amount of work.

162
00:09:16,477 --> 00:09:19,347
So we end up back at the
paradox we started with.

163
00:09:20,135 --> 00:09:23,365
Batteries did not become
dangerous. They became everywhere.

164
00:09:25,945 --> 00:09:28,005
We found a cheap, portable way to keep an

165
00:09:28,085 --> 00:09:31,275
extraordinary amount of energy
sitting perfectly still.

166
00:09:33,865 --> 00:09:35,995
The hard part was never storing it.

167
00:09:36,525 --> 00:09:38,495
It is persuading it to stay put.
